This key assumes one line per product in an order. If the domain permits repeated product lines, add a line number or use a surrogate line id and keep the required business uniqueness separately.

```sql
-- Surrogate key: a system-generated id with no business meaning
CREATE TABLE users (
    id    BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,  -- surrogate
    email VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE         -- natural value kept unique
);

-- Natural key: use the real-world value directly as the primary key
CREATE TABLE countries (
    iso_code CHAR(2) PRIMARY KEY,               -- natural, controlled, meaningful
    name     VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL
);
```

A surrogate key is useful when natural identifiers might change, such as an email address. A compact natural key can also work when its owner, stability, and change process are clear.

```sql
-- WRONG: email (a natural key) is the primary key
CREATE TABLE users (
    email VARCHAR(255) PRIMARY KEY,
    name  VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
);
CREATE TABLE posts (
    id     BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    author VARCHAR(255) REFERENCES users(email)   -- FK on the email value
);

-- One email change forces an update across every referencing row
UPDATE users SET email = 'new@x.com' WHERE email = 'old@x.com';
-- ...and every posts.author row must change too, or the FK breaks
```

```sql
-- A designed-to-be-stable surrogate id is the key; email is UNIQUE
CREATE TABLE users (
    id    BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    email VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE
);
CREATE TABLE posts (
    id        BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    author_id BIGINT REFERENCES users(id)          -- FK on the surrogate id
);

-- Changing the email touches one row and not the referencing rows
UPDATE users SET email = 'new@x.com' WHERE id = 1;
```

Foreign keys now point at the surrogate id, so an email change does not require rewriting the referencing rows. The schema does not make the id immutable automatically; the application and permissions should protect identity changes.

```sql
-- PostgreSQL identity: small and ordered
CREATE TABLE orders (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY
);

-- PostgreSQL UUID: independently generated and stored in its native type
CREATE TABLE events (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY
);
```

These are two valid designs. Pick after deciding where ids are created and checking the index and storage trade-offs on the database you actually use.
